    <!-- https://github.com/Tao-AI-group/COMBINI#000594 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://github.com/Tao-AI-group/COMBINI#000594">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://github.com/Tao-AI-group/COMBINI#000593"/>
        <rdfs:comment>Source: National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NCCIH)
  https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/green-tea</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Source: UMLS 
  https://uts.nlm.nih.gov/uts/umls/concept/C1384640</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Green_Tea</rdfs:label>
        <core:definition>Tea derived from the dried leaves of the plant Camellia sinensis with potential antioxidant, chemopreventive, and lipid-lowering activities. Green tea contains polyphenols that are believed to be responsible for its chemopreventive effect. The polyphenol fraction contains mainly Epigallocatechin-3-gallate (EGCG) and other catechins, such as epicatechin (EC), gallocatechin gallate (GCG), epigallocatechin (EGC), and epicatechin gallate (ECG). Green tea polyphenols act as antioxidants and free radical scavengers and may affect enzymes involved in cellular replication and tumor angiogenesis by modulating angiogenic factors, such as vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF). (NCI)</core:definition>
        <core:definition>Tea derived from the dried leaves of the plant Camellia sinensis with potential antioxidant, chemopreventive, and lipid-lowering activities. Green tea contains polyphenols that may be responsible for its chemopreventive effect. The polyphenol fraction contains mainly epigallocatechin-3-gallate (EGCG) and other catechins, such as epicatechin (EC), gallocatechin gallate (GCG), epigallocatechin (EGC), and epicatechin gallate (ECG). Green tea polyphenols act as antioxidants and free radical scavengers and may affect enzymes involved in cellular replication and tumor angiogenesis by modulating angiogenic factors, such as vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF). Check for &quot;https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/clinical-trials/intervention/C67048&quot; active clinical trials using this agent. (&quot;http://ncit.nci.nih.gov/ncitbrowser/ConceptReport.jsp?dictionary=NCI%20Thesaurus&amp;code=C67048&quot; NCI Thesaurus) (PDQ)</core:definition>
        <UMLS_CUI>C1384640</UMLS_CUI>
        <UMLS_Semantic_Type>Food</UMLS_Semantic_Type>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- https://github.com/Tao-AI-group/COMBINI#000595 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="https://github.com/Tao-AI-group/COMBINI#000595">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://github.com/Tao-AI-group/COMBINI#000593"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://github.com/Tao-AI-group/BSO_AD#R_04009"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="https://github.com/Tao-AI-group/COMBINI#000581"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:comment>Source: UMLS 
  https://uts.nlm.nih.gov/uts/umls/concept/C2981284</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Sage_Tea</rdfs:label>
        <core:definition>An herbal tea made from the dried leaves of Salvia officinalis, a small perennial evergreen. Sage leaves contain tannic acid, oleic acid, ursonic acid, ursolic acid, cornsole, cornsolic acid, fumaric acid, chlorogenic acid, caffeic acid, niacin, nicotinamide, flavones, flavonoid glycosides, and estrogenic substances. Extract of Sage exhibits antioxidant and radical-scavenging activities. (NCI)</core:definition>
        <UMLS_CUI>C2981284</UMLS_CUI>
        <UMLS_Semantic_Type>Chemical Viewed Functionally</UMLS_Semantic_Type>
    </owl:Class>
    


